QUESTION_PROMPT_BEGIN bytes=580
The universe is filled with the Cosmic Microwave Background. Consider the annihilation of high energy \gamma-rays with a photon from the CMB Radiation into electron-positron, i.e. $\gamma\gamma\rightarrow e^{+}e^{-}$. From what energy \gamma-rays would have their lifetimes in the universe limited by this process? Knowing that the average photon energy of the CMB is $10^{-3}eV$.

Choices:
A. 9.5*1e4 GeV
B. 2.6*1e5 GeV
C. 1.8*1e5 GeV
D. 3.9*1e5 GeV

Solve the question. At the end, write exactly one final line in this format and do not write anything after it:
Answer: <letter>
QUESTION_PROMPT_END
MODEL_OUTPUT_BEGIN bytes=922
We need the threshold energy for the process \(\gamma \gamma \to e^+ e^-\) with one photon from the CMB.  
The threshold condition in the center-of-mass frame is that the total energy is at least \(2m_e c^2\).  
For two photons with energies \(E_\gamma\) (the high-energy gamma) and \(\epsilon\) (the CMB photon), the invariant energy squared is  
\[
s = 4 E_\gamma \epsilon
\]  
in the approximation that they are head-on and neglecting small angles.  
Setting \(s \ge (2m_e c^2)^2\) gives  
\[
4 E_\gamma \epsilon \ge (2 m_e c^2)^2 = 4 m_e^2 c^4
\]  
so  
\[
E_\gamma \ge \frac{m_e^2 c^4}{\epsilon}.
\]  
With \(m_e c^2 = 0.511 \text{ MeV}\) and \(\epsilon = 10^{-3} \text{ eV}\),  
\[
E_\gamma \ge \frac{(0.511\times 10^6 \text{ eV})^2}{10^{-3} \text{ eV}} = \frac{2.61\times 10^{11} \text{ eV}^2}{10^{-3} \text{ eV}} = 2.61\times 10^{14} \text{ eV} = 2.61\times 10^5 \text{ GeV}.
\]  
That matches choice B.

Answer: B
MODEL_OUTPUT_END

