We need the diffusion coefficient for copper in aluminum at 600°C.  
Given:  
- Preexponential \( D_0 = 6.5 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{m}^2/\text{s} \)  
- Activation energy \( Q = 136,000 \, \text{J/mol} \)  
- Temperature \( T = 600^\circ\text{C} = 873.15 \, \text{K} \)  
- Gas constant \( R = 8.314 \, \text{J/(mol·K)} \)  

The diffusion coefficient is:  
\[
D = D_0 \exp\left(-\frac{Q}{RT}\right)
\]  
Compute exponent:  
\[
\frac{Q}{RT} = \frac{136000}{8.314 \times 873.15} \approx \frac{136000}{7260.5} \approx 18.73
\]  
So:  
\[
D = 6.5 \times 10^{-5} \times e^{-18.73}
\]  
\( e^{-18.73} \approx 7.5 \times 10^{-9} \) (more precisely, using calculation: \( e^{-18.73} \approx 7.2 \times 10^{-9} \)).  
Then:  
\[
D \approx 6.5 \times 10^{-5} \times 7.2 \times 10^{-9} = 4.68 \times 10^{-13} \, \text{m}^2/\text{s}
\]  
This matches closest to choice J: \( 4.7 \times 10^{-13} \).  

Answer: J
MODEL_OUTPUT_END

