Assuming the signal $x(t)=(\frac{\sin(50\pi t)}{\pi t})^2$ has a Fourier transform result of $X(j\omega)$, with a sampling frequency $\omega_s=150\pi$, the sampled signal is $g(t)$, and the Fourier transform of $g(t)$ is $G(j\omega)$. If $G(j\omega)$ is required to satisfy: $G(j\omega)=75X(j\omega) \quad for \quad |\omega|\leq\omega_0$, what is the maximum value of $\omega_0$?

Choices:
A. 50\pi
B. 37.5\pi
C. 150\pi
D. 125\pi
E. 43.75\pi
F. 62.5\pi
G. 75\pi
H. 25\pi
I. 87.5\pi
J. 100\pi

Solve the question. At the end, write exactly one final line in this format and do not write anything after it:
Answer: <letter>
QUESTION_PROMPT_END
MODEL_OUTPUT_BEGIN bytes=2287
We have  
\[
x(t) = \left( \frac{\sin(50\pi t)}{\pi t} \right)^2
\]  
The Fourier transform of \(\frac{\sin(50\pi t)}{\pi t}\) is a rectangle of width \(100\pi\) centered at zero, height \(1\). Squaring in time corresponds to convolution in frequency:  
\[
X(j\omega) = \frac{1}{2\pi} \cdot \text{rect}\left(\frac{\omega}{100\pi}\right) * \text{rect}\left(\frac{\omega}{100\pi}\right)
\]  
Convolution of two rectangles of width \(100\pi\) gives a triangle of width \(200\pi\), centered at zero, with maximum height \(100\pi\) (since area under each rectangle is \(100\pi\), and convolution peak = product of widths = \(100\pi\)). Scaling by \(1/(2\pi)\) gives peak height \(50\). So  
\[
X(j\omega) = \begin{cases}
50 - \frac{|\omega|}{2\pi}, & |\omega| \leq 100\pi \\
0, & \text{elsewhere? Actually triangle extends to } \pm 100\pi? \text{ Wait: width } 200\pi \text{ means support } |\omega| \leq 100\pi? 
\end{cases}
\]  
Let's be precise: \(\text{rect}(\omega/(100\pi))\) has support \(|\omega| \leq 50\pi\). Convolution of two such gives support \(|\omega| \leq 100\pi\), and shape:  
\[
X(j\omega) = \frac{1}{2\pi} \cdot (100\pi - |\omega|) \quad \text{for } |\omega| \leq 100\pi, \quad 0 \text{ otherwise}.
\]  
So \(X(j\omega) = 50 - \frac{|\omega|}{2\pi}\) for \(|\omega| \leq 100\pi\).

Sampling frequency \(\omega_s = 150\pi\) rad/s. Sampling period \(T = 2\pi / \omega_s = 2\pi/(150\pi) = 1/75\) s. The sampled signal \(g(t) = x(t) \cdot \sum \delta(t - nT)\). Its Fourier transform is  
\[
G(j\omega) = \frac{1}{T} \sum_{k=-\infty}^{\infty} X(j(\omega - k\omega_s)).
\]  
Here \(1/T = 75\). So  
\[
G(j\omega) = 75 \sum_{k=-\infty}^{\infty} X(j(\omega - 150\pi k)).
\]

We want \(G(j\omega) = 75 X(j\omega)\) for \(|\omega| \leq \omega_0\). This means that within that band, there is no overlap from shifted copies. The baseband copy is centered at 0, support \(|\omega| \leq 100\pi\). The nearest replicas are centered at \(\pm 150\pi\), each with support extending to \(150\pi \pm 100\pi\). The right replica starts affecting frequencies above \(150\pi - 100\pi = 50\pi\). The left replica affects frequencies below \(-50\pi\). So to have no overlap, we need \(|\omega| \leq 50\pi\). Thus maximum \(\omega_0 = 50\pi\).

Therefore answer: A. 50π.

Final line:  
Answer: A
MODEL_OUTPUT_END

