Take \(L = (7, 7\sqrt{3})\). Then \(AL = \sqrt{49 + 147} = \sqrt{196} = 14\).  
\(KL = \sqrt{(14-7)^2 + (0-7\sqrt{3})^2} = \sqrt{49 + 147} = 14\). Good.

So \(K=(14,0)\), \(L=(7,7\sqrt{3})\).

---

**Step 4 – Conditions BK=14, CL=14**  
\(B = (b,0)\). \(BK = \sqrt{(b-14)^2 + 0^2} = |b-14| = 14\).  
So \(b-14 = 14\) or \(b-14 = -14\). Since \(B\) is on positive x-axis from A, \(b>0\).  
If \(b-14 = -14\) then \(b=0\) (coincides with A, impossible). So \(b-14 = 14 \Rightarrow b=28\).

Thus \(B=(28,0)\).

---

\(C = (0,c)\). \(CL = \sqrt{(7-0)^2 + (7\sqrt{3} - c)^2} = 14\).  
So \(49 + (7\sqrt{3} - c)^2 = 196\).  
\((7\sqrt{3} - c)^2 = 147\).  
So \(7\sqrt{3} - c = \pm \sqrt{147} = \pm 7\sqrt{3}\).  
If \(7\sqrt{3} - c = 7\sqrt{3} \Rightarrow c=0\) (coincides with A).  
If \(7\sqrt{3} - c = -7\sqrt{3} \Rightarrow c = 14\sqrt{3}\).

Thus \(C = (0, 14\sqrt{3})\).

