We are given:

(1) \( 2Fe(s) + \frac{3}{2}O_2(g) \rightarrow Fe_2O_3(s) \) with \(\Delta G = -177\) kcal  
(2) \( 4Fe_2O_3(s) + Fe(s) \rightarrow 3Fe_3O_4(s) \) with \(\Delta G = -19\) kcal  

We need the Gibbs free energy of formation of \(Fe_3O_4\) (per mole). The formation reaction is:

\( 3Fe(s) + 2O_2(g) \rightarrow Fe_3O_4(s) \)

We can combine reactions to get this.

First, multiply reaction (1) by 4 to get \( 8Fe(s) + 6O_2(g) \rightarrow 4Fe_2O_3(s) \) with \(\Delta G = 4 \times (-177) = -708\) kcal.

Now add reaction (2): \( 4Fe_2O_3(s) + Fe(s) \rightarrow 3Fe_3O_4(s) \) with \(\Delta G = -19\) kcal.

Sum: \( 8Fe(s) + 6O_2(g) + Fe(s) \rightarrow 3Fe_3O_4(s) \)  
That is \( 9Fe(s) + 6O_2(g) \rightarrow 3Fe_3O_4(s) \) with \(\Delta G = -708 - 19 = -727\) kcal.

For formation of 1 mole of \(Fe_3O_4\), divide by 3:  
\(\Delta G_f = -727 / 3 \approx -242.333...\) kcal/mol.

That matches choice A: \(-243.3\) kcal/mol (likely rounding to one decimal).

Answer: A
