In 1814, 15 British individuals settled on T Island in the middle of the Atlantic Ocean, one of whom was a carrier of the recessive gene for retinitis pigmentosa. By 1960, among the 240 descendants living on this island, there were 4 patients and at least 9 carriers of the recessive gene, a proportion far higher than that in the ancestral British population. What factors caused the change in gene frequency in this population?

Choices:
A. Recessive Selection
B. Natural Migration
C. Natural Selection
D. Gene Mutations
E. Migration
F. Population Bottleneck
G. Genetic Flow
H. Genetic Drift
I. Artificial Mutation
J. Founder Effect

Solve the question. At the end, write exactly one final line in this format and do not write anything after it:
Answer: <letter>
QUESTION_PROMPT_END
MODEL_OUTPUT_BEGIN bytes=568
The scenario describes a small founding population (15 British individuals) with one carrier of a recessive gene, leading to a much higher frequency of that gene in the isolated descendant population. This is a classic example of the founder effect, where a small group establishes a new population and its gene pool differs from the source population due to chance.

The other options (genetic drift, bottleneck, migration) are related but less specific. The founder effect is a particular type of genetic drift caused by the establishment of a new colony.

Answer: J
MODEL_OUTPUT_END

